 "evidence": "Arithmetic on two ExoPlaSim runs and committed products. The two runs share the design sunlight (scale 0.970); the design case flew PlaSim's earlier longwave and liquid optics for all cloud, the test the longwave refitted by height and ice optics for cloud colder than -38 C, and neither carries the gases, the glow or their dimming. The dimming a target needs is the test's top-of-air gain per kelvin times the kelvins to shed, at the test's albedo; the climate at a target between the two runs is interpolated linearly in the ground's temperature, so it mixes the two physics and carries neither the refit's high cloud at the cooler end nor the dimming's own cut to the rain. The heat beyond the sunlight takes the GCM's efficacies for the gases and the glow and an efficacy of 1 for heat released at the ground.",
 "reading_rule": "W/m2 are means over the whole Moon; absorbed sunlight is what the Moon takes in at the top of the air. A dimming is the share of the sunlight reaching the Moon that the fleet takes away, at the test's albedo, and the sunlight scale the GCM would fly for it is the design scale times what remains. Heat at the ground is in the 3-day means at the lowest model level, about 0.9 km up, pooled over land cells and means; deserts are land under 0.5 mm of rain a day. Pairs are [low, high] ends: the two fits for the feedback, the light study's and Earth's models' ends for the costs.",
